    ("What is the basic formula for Profit and Loss percentage?", "Profit = SP - CP. Profit% = (Profit / CP) * 100. Loss = CP - SP. Loss% = (Loss / CP) * 100. Both are strictly calculated over Cost Price (CP).", "Easy", "Concept", "", "What is marked price?"),
    ("A shopkeeper buys an article for $200 and sells it for $250. What is his profit percentage?", "25%. Profit = 250 - 200 = $50. Profit% = (50 / 200) * 100 = 25%.", "Easy", "Problem Solving", "", "What if he sold it for $150?"),
    ("By selling an item for $450, a merchant loses 10%. At what price should he sell it to gain 20%?", "$600. SP = 90% of CP = 450 -> CP = 450 / 0.9 = $500. Target SP = 120% of CP = 1.20 * 500 = $600.", "Easy", "Problem Solving", "", "What was the cost price?"),
    ("A dishonest dealer claims to sell goods at cost price, but uses a 900g weight instead of a 1kg (1000g) weight. What is his gain percentage?", "11.11% (11 1/9%). Gain% = [Error / (True Value - Error)] * 100 = [100 / 900] * 100 = 11.11%.", "Intermediate", "Problem Solving", "", "What if he uses 800g weight?"),
    ("If the cost price of 12 pens is equal to the selling price of 8 pens, what is the gain percentage?", "50% gain. Let CP of 1 pen = $1 -> CP of 12 pens = $12. SP of 8 pens = $12 -> SP of 1 pen = 12/8 = $1.50. Profit per pen = $0.50. Profit% = (0.50 / 1.00) * 100 = 50%.", "Easy", "Problem Solving", "", "What is the shortcut formula: (Good - Sold)/Sold * 100?"),
    ("A trader sells two watches at $990 each. On one he gains 10% and on the other he loses 10%. What is his overall gain or loss percentage?", "Loss of 1%. When two items are sold at the same selling price with x% gain on one and x% loss on the other, there is ALWAYS an overall loss of (x/10)^2 % = (10/10)^2 = 1% loss.", "Intermediate", "Concept", "", "What is the total loss in dollar amount?"),
    ("A book is marked at $500. A discount of 20% is given. What is the selling price?", "$400. Discount = 20% of 500 = $100. Selling Price = 500 - 100 = $400.", "Easy", "Problem Solving", "", "What if an additional 10% discount is applied?"),
    ("A merchant marks his goods 40% above the cost price and allows a discount of 25% on marked price. What is his profit percentage?", "5% profit. Let CP = 100. Marked Price (MP) = 140. Discount = 25% of 140 = 35. Selling Price = 140 - 35 = 105. Profit% = (105 - 100) = 5%.", "Easy", "Problem Solving", "", "What is the markup formula?"),
    ("Find the single discount equivalent to three successive discounts of 20%, 10%, and 5%.", "31.6%. Let MP = 100. After 20%: 80. After 10%: 80 * 0.9 = 72. After 5%: 72 * 0.95 = 68.4. Equivalent discount = 100 - 68.4 = 31.6%.", "Intermediate", "Problem Solving", "", "What is equivalent discount of 50% and 50%?"),
